[~nileshk03] : Thank you for your interest. Feel free to assign the ticket to yourself.

{quote}
One thing to settle first: for MAX_TIMESTAMP, bounding the scan by position is not enough. The current UnifiedLog path first picks the segment with the globally greatest maxTimestampSoFar, then looks inside that segment. If that segment’s max timestamp is beyond the fetchable bound, we could return -1 even though the correct max timestamp exists in an earlier batch or segment.
{quote}

Yes, this is also an issue. Not sure how to address this efficiently.

{quote}
Also, since maxPosition is batch-granular and HW/LSO can fall mid-batch,
{quote}
Since replication is at batch level, HW is always at batch boundary. Ditto for LSO since firstUnstableOffset() is at batch boundary.

Claud suggested the following approach to find MAX_TIMESTAMP. If segment level MAX_TIMESTAMP is within the bound (e.g. HWM or Last Stable Offset), we can just use that.  Otherwise, use the index on the segment containing the bound to find the MAX_TIMESTAMP like the following.
{code:java}
(ts_k, off_k) = timeIndex.lastEntryWithOffsetBelow(bound);   // binary search on the monotonic offset field
(off_c, pos_c) = offsetIndex.lookup(bound);                  // last offset-index checkpoint <= bound
tailMax = scanBatchesForMax(view.slice(pos_c, posOf(bound) - pos_c));  // < index.interval.bytes
M(bound) = maxByTimestamp((ts_k, off_k), tailMax); {code}

